# log4j-core's JndiLookup resolver handed the variable text it was given to
# JndiManager.lookup(String), which passed it straight to javax.naming
# Context.lookup(String) -- so a name carrying its own scheme and authority
# ("ldap://host/a") made the JVM fetch and instantiate whatever a directory
# server outside the caller's control described. The restriction that shipped
# for this weakness (log4j-core/.../net/JndiManager.java) keeps that call and
# gates it: it parses `new URI(name)`, returns null unless the scheme is in an
# allowed-protocol list, and for ldap/ldaps additionally requires the host to be
# a local IP and the entry's javaClassName to be one of eight JDK value classes,
# rejecting javaReferenceAddress/javaFactory entries outright. It also swaps
# InitialContext for InitialDirContext so the attributes can be inspected first.
#
# javax.naming Context.lookup is already labelled code.Deserialization -- the
# concept the readObject/Yaml.load/XMLDecoder sink family carries, since a naming
# service outside the caller's control chooses what object gets instantiated --
# so what the first two cases pin is the flow, not the label: whether an
# untrusted resolver argument is still tracked across the resolver's own
# indirection, a prefix-normalising helper whose return value feeds a manager
# object's lookup, which feeds the context's. The expect case shows it is: the
# reported path runs caller -> resolver parameter -> convertJndiName parameter ->
# its return -> manager parameter -> Context.lookup argument.
#
# The reject case is the flow that remains once the resolver can no longer be
# handed variable text from a request: the resolver still exists and is still
# called on every format, but only the configuration's own constant property name
# reaches it, and the request-derived text is appended verbatim. That is a flow
# distinction rather than a control one, because restricting the name instead of
# removing the flow is not something this engine credits, and should not be
# credited here even if it could: the allowlist gate above is a branch-sensitive
# `return null` on a value derived from the same tainted variable (the
# sanitize-or-return idiom the dataflow design already records as out of scope
# for a branch-insensitive solver), and it was itself bypassable -- the following
# release removed the message-side lookup rather than narrowing the allowlist
# further. A control meaning only "a name was checked against a list" would
# endorse exactly the hole that restriction left open. Measured on the two real
# revisions of JndiManager.java plus JndiLookup.java: VYQL-DESER-001 labels the
# Context.lookup argument on both, and a repro faithful to both reports
# identically, so the restriction is not a discriminator.
#
# The other half of this weakness is the path by which a logged string becomes
# that name, and it is not traceable. A formatted message reaches
# StrSubstitutor, which asks Interpolator to resolve each `${prefix:name}`, and
# Interpolator dispatches with `strLookupMap.get(prefix).lookup(event, name)` --
# the receiver is a StrLookup, an interface with about fifteen implementations
# registered by prefix. A call whose receiver's type resolves to an interface or
# abstract type is deliberately left unrouted, so no implementation's body is
# entered and a sink inside one is unreachable; only a receiver whose type is a
# concrete class reaches it. The last two cases isolate that, and deliberately
# not on a lookup sink: the javax.naming sink pattern matches the bare method
# name `lookup` whenever the receiver's type cannot be proven (recv_type unknown
# is treated as "cannot disprove"), so the registry call would be labelled a sink
# itself and hide the missing hop. With Runtime.exec instead, the pair differs in
# the receiver's declared type alone -- the same registry read, the same single
# implementation, the same argument.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention, the restriction is not a discriminator and
# the message-to-resolver hop is an engine gap; cases three and four hold the
# isolating pair for that gap.

test "rank1339: a resolver passes caller-supplied variable text through to Context.lookup"
  lang java
  file JndiLookup.java
  expect VYQL-DESER-001
  code
  ```
  import java.util.Objects;

  import javax.naming.Context;
  import javax.naming.InitialContext;
  import javax.naming.NamingException;

  class JndiManager {
    private final Context context;

    private JndiManager(final Context context) {
      this.context = context;
    }

    public static JndiManager getDefaultManager() throws NamingException {
      return new JndiManager(new InitialContext());
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> T lookup(final String name) throws NamingException {
      return (T) this.context.lookup(name);
    }
  }

  class JndiLookup {
    static final String CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX = "java:comp/env/";

    public String lookup(final String key) {
      if (key == null) {
        return null;
      }
      final String jndiName = convertJndiName(key);
      try {
        final JndiManager jndiManager = JndiManager.getDefaultManager();
        return Objects.toString(jndiManager.lookup(jndiName), null);
      } catch (final NamingException e) {
        return null;
      }
    }

    private String convertJndiName(final String jndiName) {
      if (!jndiName.startsWith(CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX) && jndiName.indexOf(':') == -1) {
        return CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX + jndiName;
      }
      return jndiName;
    }
  }

  class VersionServlet extends HttpServlet {
    private final JndiLookup resolver = new JndiLookup();

    public void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, StringBuilder toAppendTo) {
      final String message = request.getHeader("X-Api-Version");
      toAppendTo.append(resolver.lookup(message));
    }
  }

  class HttpServlet {}
  interface HttpServletRequest { String getHeader(String name); }
  ```

test "rank1339: a resolver reached only by a configured property name, with request text appended verbatim"
  lang java
  file JndiLookup.java
  reject VYQL-DESER-001
  code
  ```
  import java.util.Objects;

  import javax.naming.Context;
  import javax.naming.InitialContext;
  import javax.naming.NamingException;

  class JndiManager {
    private final Context context;

    private JndiManager(final Context context) {
      this.context = context;
    }

    public static JndiManager getDefaultManager() throws NamingException {
      return new JndiManager(new InitialContext());
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> T lookup(final String name) throws NamingException {
      return (T) this.context.lookup(name);
    }
  }

  class JndiLookup {
    static final String CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX = "java:comp/env/";

    public String lookup(final String key) {
      if (key == null) {
        return null;
      }
      final String jndiName = convertJndiName(key);
      try {
        final JndiManager jndiManager = JndiManager.getDefaultManager();
        return Objects.toString(jndiManager.lookup(jndiName), null);
      } catch (final NamingException e) {
        return null;
      }
    }

    private String convertJndiName(final String jndiName) {
      if (!jndiName.startsWith(CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX) && jndiName.indexOf(':') == -1) {
        return CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX + jndiName;
      }
      return jndiName;
    }
  }

  class VersionServlet extends HttpServlet {
    private static final String VERSION_PROPERTY = "java:comp/env/appVersion";

    private final JndiLookup resolver = new JndiLookup();

    public void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, StringBuilder toAppendTo) {
      final String message = request.getHeader("X-Api-Version");
      toAppendTo.append(message);
      toAppendTo.append(resolver.lookup(VERSION_PROPERTY));
    }
  }

  class HttpServlet {}
  interface HttpServletRequest { String getHeader(String name); }
  ```

test "rank1339: a registry handler held at its concrete type carries taint into its body"
  lang java
  file Dispatcher.java
  expect VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  import java.util.HashMap;
  import java.util.Map;

  interface Handler {
    void run(String cmd) throws Exception;
  }

  class ExecHandler implements Handler {
    public void run(final String cmd) throws Exception {
      Runtime.getRuntime().exec(cmd);
    }
  }

  class Dispatcher extends HttpServlet {
    private final Map<String, Handler> handlers = new HashMap<>();

    Dispatcher() {
      handlers.put("exec", new ExecHandler());
    }

    public void doGet(HttpServletRequest request) throws Exception {
      final String t = request.getParameter("t");
      final ExecHandler handler = (ExecHandler) handlers.get("exec");
      handler.run(t);
    }
  }

  class HttpServlet {}
  interface HttpServletRequest { String getParameter(String name); }
  ```

